#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""haproxywho - which backend would serve Host H path P, and which
`use_backend` line decided it.

A static model of HAProxy's documented routing rules
(DESIGN-haproxywho.md, BUILD APPROVED 2026-11-09). Semantics pinned
to the HAProxy Configuration Manual, 2.6 LTS - 3.2 range. Claims are
tagged [documented], [docs-inferred], or [hypothesized]; no haproxy
binary exists on this box, so no claim here is [observed] and there
is no live-agreement test to skip-guard against: `haproxy -c`
validates syntax but dumps no routing decisions, so hermetic
fixture trees are the only oracle.

Pinned semantics (HA01-HA24 in the design doc):
- HA01 frontend selection is by bind socket, never by Host. A bare
  query is evaluated in every HTTP frontend; agreement is the
  answer, disagreement is BIND-AMBIGUOUS. --port/--bind restrict.
- HA02 mode resolves own, then defaults chain, else TCP (the
  default). Only mode http frontends get verdicts.
- HA03 defaults scope preceding-only and reset at the next defaults
  section; named selection via `from` (2.4+) chains transitively.
  ACLs never live in defaults; use_backend never does either.
- HA04 ACLs are section-local, name-accumulating (OR across lines),
  case-sensitive; forward references resolve.
- HA05 condition grammar: [if|unless] term [term ...] [or ...];
  terms ANDed, `or` separates AND-groups; `!` negates one term;
  anonymous `{ ... }` ACLs; predefined TRUE/FALSE/METH_*/LOCALHOST/
  HTTP.
- HA06 first matching use_backend wins (declaration order), else
  default_backend (own, else defaults), else listen-self, else
  NO-BACKEND (503).
- HA07 statically evaluable fetch set: hdr(host), path, pathq, url,
  method, src, dst, dst_port, *_len, always_true/false. Anything
  else is HA12.
- HA08 Host matched verbatim, port included; a port-stripped check
  is shown only as a displayed alternative, never the verdict.
- HA09 path is verbatim, query excluded from `path`, included in
  `pathq`/`url`; no normalization.
- HA10 three-valued (Kleene) conditions: TRUE/FALSE/UNKNOWN.
  Unknown terms poison a verdict only when they could change the
  winner.
- HA11 `-f` pattern files expanded when readable (relative to the
  config directory); missing/unreadable is unmodeled, never an
  empty pattern list. `virt@` and `-M` maps are unmodeled.
- HA12 unmodeled dynamism (maps, stick tables, ssl_fc, var(),
  converters, ...) is bannered and UNKNOWN-poisoning, never
  approximated.
- HA13 http-request rewrites run BEFORE backend selection: a
  rewrite that may have fired makes the verdict UNKNOWN, citing
  the rule. HA14 terminating rules (deny/redirect/return/tarpit/
  silent-drop/block) short-circuit the request.
- HA15 regex is an approximation: POSIX classes translated to
  Python re where they coincide; untranslatable patterns are
  UNKNOWN; regex-decided verdicts carry [regex-approx].
- HA16-HA24 structural findings, coverage on every run, exact
  verdict vocabulary, exit codes 0/1/2.

The tool never runs haproxy, never opens a socket, never writes,
never reads the process environment (HA18), and opens config and
`-f` pattern files read-only.

Exit codes: 0 verdict BACKEND rendered (default-fallback and
listen-self included, labeled), or --list-frontends with >=1 proxy;
1 NO-BACKEND, SHORT-CIRCUIT, BIND-AMBIGUOUS, NO-FRONTEND,
NO-HTTP-FRONTEND, or UNKNOWN; 2 usage error, missing config,
.include cycle, a file opening mid-section, or a --path not
starting with `/`.
"""

